  This note describes a Monte Carlo option pricer written in Python and the tests used to check it. The underlying follows a geometric Brownian motion under the risk-neutral measure, simulated exactly on a time grid. The pricer covers European calls and puts, arithmetic Asian options and discretely monitored barrier options, and estimates delta, gamma and vega by central finite differences with common random numbers. Every estimate carries a standard error, and every check is statistical. European prices agree with the Black–Scholes formula in twelve scenarios, with a largest deviation of #R.scenarios.max_abs_z standard errors; the one value outside its 95% interval is shown to be chance by repeating the test. Asian and barrier prices agree with independent closed-form references, and finite-difference Greeks with their analytic values. No novelty is claimed for the methods, which are textbook material; the contribution is a tested and reproducible implementation.
]

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

= Introduction

A Monte Carlo pricer computes the price of a derivative as the discounted expectation of its payoff under the risk-neutral measure, approximated by an average over simulated scenarios. The approach needs nothing more than the payoff evaluated on a simulated path, so a single engine prices European, Asian and barrier options alike. It has two weaknesses. The statistical error decreases only as $1\/sqrt(N)$ in the number of paths $N$, and an estimate can be precise without being correct: an error in the model or in a payoff produces a wrong number with a small standard error.

This note describes such a pricer and, above all, how it was tested. The strategy is to validate the engine where exact answers are known before relying on it where they are not. Every Monte Carlo number is reported with its standard error SE, and agreement with a reference value $V$ is measured by the z-score
$ z = (hat(V) - V) / "SE", $
which is approximately standard normal when the implementation is correct: $|z| < 1.96$ in about 95% of independent tests, while values far beyond 3 point to an error.

@sec:model describes the model, the estimator and the structure of the code. @sec:validation validates European prices against the Black–Scholes formula. @sec:exotics prices Asian and barrier options, whose payoffs depend on the whole path, and checks them against independent references. @sec:greeks estimates Greeks by finite differences. @sec:limitations states what is not new and the limitations of the implementation, and @app:reproduce explains how to reproduce the results. Every number and figure in this note is produced by a single script from fixed random seeds.

= Model and estimator <sec:model>

== Risk-neutral dynamics

Under the risk-neutral measure $QQ$ the price of the underlying follows a geometric Brownian motion,
$ d S_t = r S_t thin d t + sigma S_t thin d W_t, $
with constant risk-free rate $r$ and volatility $sigma$. Its solution gives the exact update over a step of length $tau$:
$ S_(t + tau) = S_t exp[(r - 1/2 sigma^2) tau + sigma sqrt(tau) thin Z], quad Z tilde cal(N)(0, 1). $
The term $-1/2 sigma^2$ is the Itô correction that makes $EE^QQ [S_T] = S_0 e^(r T)$. Paths are simulated on the grid $t_i = i T\/n$, $i = 0, dots, n$, with independent normal draws at each step. The scheme has no discretization error at the grid points, so a European payoff, which depends only on $S_T$, needs a single step. For the path-dependent contracts of @sec:exotics the grid is part of the contract: $n$ is the number of monitoring dates.

Unless stated otherwise, $S_0 = #R.parameters.S0$, $K = #R.parameters.K$, $r = #R.parameters.r$, $sigma = #R.parameters.sigma$ and $T = #R.parameters.T$ year.

== Estimator and standard error

With $N$ simulated paths and discounted payoffs $X_i = e^(-r T) thin "payoff"_i$, the price estimate and its standard error are
$ hat(V) = 1/N sum_(i=1)^N X_i, quad "SE" = s / sqrt(N), $
where $s$ is the sample standard deviation of the $X_i$. By the central limit theorem, $hat(V) plus.minus 1.96 thin "SE"$ is an approximate 95% confidence interval. Halving the standard error requires four times as many paths.

== Structure of the code

The package separates the three questions that a pricer must answer:
- the *model* (`GBM`) describes how the underlying moves, and returns a matrix of simulated paths;
- an *instrument* (`EuropeanCall`, `AsianOption`, `BarrierOption`, …) describes what the contract pays on each path;
- the *engine* (`price_monte_carlo`) combines the two and returns the estimate with its standard error and confidence interval.

The engine never inspects the type of contract, so the exotic options of @sec:exotics were added as new instrument classes without changing it. Separate modules provide the Black–Scholes formulas, the validation routines and the Greeks. A complete pricing call reads:

```
model = GBM(S0=100, r=0.05, sigma=0.20)
option = BarrierOption(K=100, T=1, barrier=130,
                       kind="call", direction="up", knock="out")
result = price_monte_carlo(model, option,
                           n_paths=100_000, n_steps=252, seed=42)
```

= Validation against Black–Scholes <sec:validation>

For European options the Black–Scholes formula [1] gives the exact price under the model of @sec:model:
$ C = S_0 N(d_1) - K e^(-r T) N(d_2), quad P = K e^(-r T) N(-d_2) - S_0 N(-d_1), $
$ d_2 = (ln(S_0 \/ K) + (r - 1/2 sigma^2) T) / (sigma sqrt(T)), quad d_1 = d_2 + sigma sqrt(T), $
where $N$ is the standard normal distribution function. The implementation of the formulas was first checked without Monte Carlo: put–call parity, $C - P = S_0 - K e^(-r T)$, holds on #R.parity.cases random inputs with a largest discrepancy of #R.parity.mantissa × 10#super[#R.parity.exponent].

*Base case.* With $N = #R.base.N$ paths the Monte Carlo prices are #R.base.call.mc ± #R.base.call.se for the call and #R.base.put.mc ± #R.base.put.se for the put (estimate ± SE), against Black–Scholes prices of #R.base.call.bs and #R.base.put.bs;; the z-scores are #R.base.call.z and #R.base.put.z.

*Twelve scenarios.* A single at-the-money test is weak: with $S_0 = K$ some errors are invisible, for instance exchanging $S_0$ and $K$ in the call formula leaves its value unchanged. @tab:scenarios repeats the comparison for in- and out-of-the-money strikes, high volatility, and short and long maturities. The largest deviation is #R.scenarios.max_abs_z standard errors, and #R.scenarios.outside of the #R.scenarios.count Black–Scholes prices lies outside the 95% confidence interval: the call with $K = 80$, with $z = $ #R.scenarios.rows.at(2).z.

