= Path-dependent options <sec:exotics>

== Contracts

*Asian option.* The payoff depends on the arithmetic average of the prices observed at the monitoring dates, $A = 1/n sum_(i=1)^n S_(t_i)$, and the call pays $max(A - K, 0)$. A sum of lognormal variables is not lognormal, and no closed-form price is available.

*Barrier options.* A knock-out option becomes worthless if the underlying reaches a barrier $B$ during its life; a knock-in option comes into existence only if it does. Otherwise they pay as the corresponding European option. Here the barrier is monitored on the simulation dates, as in contracts that refer to daily closing prices. On every path exactly one of the knock-in and knock-out options is alive, so their prices add up to the European price (in–out parity).

A path matrix with a single step contains only $S_0$ and $S_T$, and a path-dependent payoff computed from it would be silently wrong: the average would reduce to $S_T$, and crossings of the barrier during the life would be missed. The Asian and barrier classes therefore reject simulations with fewer than two steps.

== Prices

@tab:exotics lists prices for daily monitoring ($n = #R.exotics.steps$) with $N = #R.exotics.N$ paths. Knock-in and knock-out prices computed from the same paths add up exactly to the European prices (the difference is #R.exotics.parity_gap in floating point), and the European call simulated on the #R.exotics.steps\-step grid remains consistent with Black–Scholes ($z = $ #R.exotics.european_252_z). The Asian call costs #R.exotics.asian_ratio of the European call.

