= Greeks <sec:greeks>

== Finite differences with common random numbers

Greeks measure how the price responds to the model parameters. Without a formula to differentiate, they are approximated by central finite differences,
$ Delta approx (V(S_0 + h) - V(S_0 - h)) / (2h), quad Gamma approx (V(S_0 + h) - 2V(S_0) + V(S_0 - h)) / h^2, $
$ cal(V) approx (V(sigma + k) - V(sigma - k)) / (2k), $
with $h = 0.01 thin S_0$ and $k = 0.01$. The difficulty is noise: $V(S_0 + h)$ and $V(S_0 - h)$ are estimates of two nearly equal numbers, and dividing their statistical errors by $2h$, or by $h^2$, amplifies them. The remedy is to simulate all bumped prices with the same normal draws, known as common random numbers [3, Ch. 7]. Since $"Var"(X - Y) = "Var"(X) + "Var"(Y) - 2 "Cov"(X, Y)$ and the covariance is then close to its maximum, most of the noise cancels in the difference. The differences are taken path by path, which yields a standard error for each Greek.

== European options

For the European call the Greeks are known in closed form: $Delta = N(d_1)$, $Gamma = phi(d_1) \/ (S_0 sigma sqrt(T))$ and $cal(V) = S_0 phi(d_1) sqrt(T)$, where $phi$ is the standard normal density. @tab:greeks-european compares them with the finite-difference estimates; the largest deviation is #R.greeks_european.max_abs_z standard errors. Repeating the computation with a different seed for each bumped simulation leaves the estimates unbiased, but multiplies the standard errors by the factors in the last column. Matching the precision of common random numbers with independent seeds would require #R.greeks_european.rows.at(0).paths_factor, #R.greeks_european.rows.at(1).paths_factor and #R.greeks_european.rows.at(2).paths_factor times as many paths for delta, gamma and vega respectively.

