The gamma of the up-and-out call shows the limits of finite differences. Its payoff jumps from as much as $B - K = 30$ to zero when the barrier is touched, so a bump of $S_0$ changes the payoff only on the few paths whose maximum lies close to the barrier, but on those paths by a large amount. With $h = 0.01 thin S_0$ the estimate is #R.greeks_exotic.bumps.at(0).gamma ± #R.greeks_exotic.bumps.at(0).gamma_se, with a standard error larger than the value itself; with $h = 0.05 thin S_0$ it is #R.greeks_exotic.bumps.at(2).gamma ± #R.greeks_exotic.bumps.at(2).gamma_se, close to the reference #R.greeks_exotic.up_out_reference.gamma (@tab:bumps). A larger bump reduces the variance at the cost of a larger approximation bias.

