= What is not new, and limitations <sec:limitations>

*Not new.* Everything implemented here is textbook material: Monte Carlo pricing and finite-difference Greeks with common random numbers [3], the Black–Scholes formula [1], closed-form barrier prices [2, 4], the continuity correction for discrete monitoring [5] and the price of the geometric Asian option [6].

*What this work consists of.* An implementation in which models, contracts and the pricing engine are independent, so that new contracts leave the engine unchanged; and a validation protocol in which every estimate carries a standard error, agreement is judged by z-scores, an apparent failure is re-examined with independent seeds rather than hidden, and path-dependent prices are checked against independent references. All results can be reproduced with one script (@app:reproduce).

*Limitations.* The implementation has four main limitations.
- The model has constant volatility and interest rate and no dividends, and it is not calibrated to market prices; volatility smiles and term structures are outside its scope.
- Paths are stored in full, so memory grows as $N n$: #R.exotics.N paths of #(R.exotics.steps + 1) prices take about 200~MB per array.
- Finite-difference Greeks of discontinuous payoffs are noisy (@sec:greeks-exotic); pathwise or likelihood-ratio estimators [3, Ch. 7] would be more suitable.
- Apart from common random numbers, no variance reduction is used; antithetic sampling and control variates [3, Ch. 4] are the natural next step.

*Provenance.* The code and this note were developed in working sessions with an AI language model (Claude Opus 5, Anthropic) under the author's direction. All numbers and figures are produced by the accompanying script from fixed seeds, and were checked as described in the preceding sections.

