= Reproducing the results <app:reproduce>

#if repo != none [
  The code is available at #link(repo)[#raw(repo)].
] else [
  The code is available from the author on request.
]
The project contains the package `option_pricer` (models, instruments, pricing engine, Black–Scholes formulas, validation routines, Greeks and plotting style), three notebooks that present the same material step by step, and the folder `report`, which holds this note, the script that produces its numbers and figures, and the font used in both. From the root of the project:

```
python3 -m pip install -r requirements.txt
python3 report/build_assets.py
typst compile --root . report/report.typ report/option_pricer_report.pdf
```

The script writes the figures to `report/figures` and every number quoted in this note to `report/results.json`, which the document reads when it is compiled; no number in the text is typed by hand. It runs in about 20 seconds on a laptop. The results above were obtained with Python #R.environment.python, NumPy #R.environment.numpy, SciPy #R.environment.scipy, Matplotlib #R.environment.matplotlib and Typst 0.15.1.

All random numbers come from NumPy's default generator with fixed seeds: #R.parameters.seed for the main results, $#R.parameters.seed + i$ for scenario $i$ of @tab:scenarios, $1000 + j$ for the repeated runs of @sec:validation and $2000 + r$ for the reference checks of @sec:reference-checks. Running the script again reproduces every number exactly.
